  "name": "Question 2",
  "problem": "A quadratic equation in the variable $x$ with real coefficients has a root $x=-\\frac{2\\text{i}}{3}.$ Given that the coefficient of ${x}^{2}$ in the equation is $-27,$ find the equation. Write your answer in the form $a{x}^{2}+bx+c=0,$ where $a,$ $b,$ and $c$ are real constants.\n\n{{field-1}}$=0$",
  "worked_solution": "Since the quadratic equation has real coefficients and $x=-\\frac{2\\text{i}}{3}$ is a root, its conjugate $x=\\frac{2\\text{i}}{3}$ must also be a root. Therefore, using the zero product property, we can write a quadratic equation with the required roots as follows:\n\n$$\n(x-\\frac{2\\text{i}}{3})(x+\\frac{2\\text{i}}{3})=0\n$$\n\nMultiplying out the parentheses and simplifying gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-{(\\frac{2\\text{i}}{3})}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-\\frac{4}{9}(-1) & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+\\frac{4}{9} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation has roots $x=\\pm \\frac{2\\text{i}}{3},$ but the coefficient of ${x}^{2}$ is not $-27.$ Therefore, to find the desired equation, we multiply the equation above by $-27.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-27({x}^{2}+\\frac{4}{9}) & =0 \\\\ -27{x}^{2}-12 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
